WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:04.440
Film Courage: At some point thought you made
the decision that you were going to write

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:05.870
and the books and teach courses.

00:00:05.870 --> 00:00:07.720
When did you write tTHE 90 DAY SCREENPLAY:
From Concept To Polish again?

00:00:07.720 --> 00:00:11.480
Alan Watt, Author, Screenwriter and Founder
of L.A. Writers’ Lab: The 90 Day novel came

00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:21.420
out in 2010, the 90 Day screenplay was 2012-2013
something like that?

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:26.660
Film Courage: Is there a chapter in the 90
Day screenplay book that most contested?

00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:29.749
Or people have the most questions about (one
part of the process)?

00:00:29.749 --> 00:00:34.180
Alan: I’m not really aware of anything being
particularly contested?

00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:36.910
I get a lot of people who love it.

00:00:36.910 --> 00:00:49.320
But it is I think than a lot of the screenwriting
books out there.

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:55.910
My perception of it is that it’s very much
about process and less about results.

00:00:55.910 --> 00:01:03.120
I think when we lose ourselves in the process
the results can take care of themselves.

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:06.939
One of the things that I teach...that’s
my cat.

00:01:06.939 --> 00:01:13.320
I don’t know if you are picking it up?...There’s
Captain.

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:21.520
But one of the things that I talk about is
how dilemma is a source of the story.

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:26.520
A lot of writing books talk about the dramatic
problem.

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:35.220
The implication with that for me and why I
was resistant to a lot of the writing books

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:39.290
that I’ve read is that problems are solved.

00:01:39.290 --> 00:01:42.340
They are intellectual (you solve the problem).

00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:45.920
Dilemmas are resolved through shifts in perception.

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:53.659
So something that I didn’t discover this
but sort of drilled down into it is that the

00:01:53.659 --> 00:01:56.830
source of our story is our protagonist dilemma.

00:01:56.830 --> 00:02:01.490
A dilemma is a problem that can’t be solved
without creating another problem.

00:02:01.490 --> 00:02:07.539
You know you’ve got a story when what the
protagonist wants is impossible to achieve

00:02:07.539 --> 00:02:11.110
based on their current approach or their current
identity.

00:02:11.110 --> 00:02:13.970
In other words it’s going to necessitate
a shift in perception.

00:02:13.970 --> 00:02:20.330
They are going to have to become a new person
so to speak by the end of the story.

00:02:20.330 --> 00:02:27.520
When you think about problems you think about
I have a flat tire.

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:31.080
I call the tow truck, they solve my problem.

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:32.810
Not an interesting story.

00:02:32.810 --> 00:02:37.760
But if I have a flat tire and I’m out in
the desert and I call the tow truck and I’m

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:42.190
dying of thirst and this guy drives out to
the desert and it’s the guy who is sleeping

00:02:42.190 --> 00:02:46.530
with my wife, now I’ve got an interesting
story.

00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:51.060
Now I want to hurt the man who is here to
save my life.

00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:57.410
When you can connect to your protagonist dilemma
then you’ve got something to work with.

00:02:57.410 --> 00:03:00.160
The dilemma is the source of your story.

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:07.660
If you look at any great story, you’ve got
a clear dilemma and when you connect to that,

00:03:07.660 --> 00:03:09.260
then you’ve got something to work with.

00:03:09.260 --> 00:03:13.840
So that’s one of the things that I help
writers with is understanding the dilemma

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:16.160
but it’s more of a holistic way.

00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:22.290
It’s not like let’s figure out your premise.

00:03:22.290 --> 00:03:30.110
The premise is born out of the protagonist’s
dilemma.

00:03:30.110 --> 00:03:37.240
For me it’s a more organic way of working
and then you start to see how plot emerges

00:03:37.240 --> 00:03:45.050
out of the character’s struggle and I’m
not talking about intellectually the dilemma

00:03:45.050 --> 00:03:49.220
because you can’t (problems are solved)
you can’t.

00:03:49.220 --> 00:03:53.340
When you try and figure out your protagonist
dilemma you get stuck.

00:03:53.340 --> 00:04:00.660
But when you start to connect to the experience
of the dilemma all sort of images and ideas

00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:06.000
and situations start to emerge to support
that.

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:12.930
So sometimes people call that the brainstorming
part of the process.

00:04:12.930 --> 00:04:18.590
The first part of the 90-day screenplay is
just doing a lot of free writing imagining

00:04:18.590 --> 00:04:24.090
your character or your protagonist in relationship
to other characters before you start to put

00:04:24.090 --> 00:04:29.190
a framework around it or insist on any kind
of structure for it.

00:04:29.190 --> 00:04:33.790
Film Courage: Do you think some people get
very confused thinking their dilemma is their

00:04:33.790 --> 00:04:34.790
problem and it’s not?

00:04:34.790 --> 00:04:36.900
Like when you were talking to the prisoners
[all taught writing a few times in a prison],

00:04:36.900 --> 00:04:39.170
were they very clear about what they thought
the problem was in their story?

00:04:39.170 --> 00:04:40.740
Maybe you didn’t get into all that with
them?

00:04:40.740 --> 00:04:44.500
Al: I don’t know if I got into all that?

00:04:44.500 --> 00:04:46.320
I can’t remember that.

00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:52.540
But sometimes writers get confused about the
difference between problem and dilemma.

00:04:52.540 --> 00:04:56.620
And you know, problems are solved.

00:04:56.620 --> 00:05:02.240
So like I say that’s solvable.

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:07.720
It’s an aspect of the dilemma.

00:05:07.720 --> 00:05:15.290
In other words, part of the story could be
that I get a flat tire and I have to get my

00:05:15.290 --> 00:05:25.380
flat tire fixed but there’s a movement to
a scene.

00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:32.370
If a scene begins with a problem like I’ve
got a flat tire and at the end of the scene

00:05:32.370 --> 00:05:37.340
I get my flat tire fixed and nothing else
happens, we don’t have a story.

00:05:37.340 --> 00:05:43.310
What has to happen is by the end of the scene
I can get my flat tire fixed but something

00:05:43.310 --> 00:05:46.920
else happens that’s going to lead the story
in a new direction.

00:05:46.920 --> 00:05:50.450
In other words, my dilemma wasn’t the flat
tire.

00:05:50.450 --> 00:05:55.920
The dilemma is my false belief about what
the flat tire means.

00:05:55.920 --> 00:06:01.940
I don’t know if that makes sense?

00:06:01.940 --> 00:06:10.030
Here is where writers get stuck, writers think
(when you hear the term dramatic problem)

00:06:10.030 --> 00:06:18.020
you may assume that it’s your job to figure
out your character’s solution to their problem.

00:06:18.020 --> 00:06:22.860
Einstein says you can’t solve a problem
at the same level of consciousness that created

00:06:22.860 --> 00:06:25.350
the problem.

00:06:25.350 --> 00:06:27.700
In other words, it’s not difficult, it’s
impossible.

00:06:27.700 --> 00:06:33.330
You are never going to figure out your protagonists
transformation.

00:06:33.330 --> 00:06:39.490
So what you don’t want to do is think that
your character has a clean line from the beginning

00:06:39.490 --> 00:06:41.190
to the end of the story.

00:06:41.190 --> 00:06:46.840
You need to understand that your protagonist
is going to die at the end of Act 2.

00:06:46.840 --> 00:06:49.090
They are going to die to their own identity.

00:06:49.090 --> 00:06:52.730
I don’t mean they are physically going to
die.

00:06:52.730 --> 00:06:59.860
But they are going to be forced to surrender
to the meaning they made out of their goal.

00:06:59.860 --> 00:07:03.960
When Jimmy Stewart leaves Bedford Falls, then
he’ll have a wonderful life.

00:07:03.960 --> 00:07:08.820
The end of Act 2 is I’m worth more dead
than alive.

00:07:08.820 --> 00:07:11.040
So he dies to his old identity.

00:07:11.040 --> 00:07:19.030
It’s a great moment where he jumps off of
the bridge (he wants to end his life).

00:07:19.030 --> 00:07:21.240
He wants to jump into the icy water.

00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:29.940
Now I love this image because Clarence jumps
into the icy water and so the action is the

00:07:29.940 --> 00:07:32.220
same.

00:07:32.220 --> 00:07:39.460
Nobody ever questions this but he jumps into
save somebody else so he doesn’t die he’s

00:07:39.460 --> 00:07:41.960
reborn and that’s the beginning of Act 3.

